From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unom <packages@unom.io>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 11:13:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 45/46] pipewire: put a planar capture's chroma on a 16-row
 boundary on Intel

Intel's media driver encodes an imported NV12 or P010 picture without a
copy, and its encoder reads the chroma from a row that is a multiple of
16, whatever row the import names. A 1080-row capture has its chroma at
row 1080, so the encoder read from row 1088, 8 chroma rows late: colour
sat 16 picture rows too high and the last 16 came out green. At 900 rows
it read 12 rows late. 720 and 1200 rows were right.

On Intel, allocate an exported two-plane LINEAR image with its height
rounded up to 16. The driver then lays the second plane on such a row and
the export names it there. The texture keeps the capture's own height, so
nothing paints or reads the rows in between.
---
 src/rendervulkan.cpp | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/rendervulkan.cpp b/src/rendervulkan.cpp
index abfd001..af14691 100644
--- a/src/rendervulkan.cpp
+++ b/src/rendervulkan.cpp
@@ -2622,6 +2622,11 @@ bool CVulkanTexture::BInit( uint32_t width, uint32_t height, uint32_t depth, uin
 		};
 	}
 
+	// Intel's encoder reads an imported picture's chroma from a row that is a multiple of 16.
+	// A taller image puts the second plane there; the rows in between are never painted.
+	if ( bExportsLinear && ( drmFormat == DRM_FORMAT_NV12 || drmFormat == DRM_FORMAT_P010 ) )
+		imageInfo.extent.height = ( height + 15 ) & ~15u;
+
 	m_width = width;
 	m_height = height;
 	m_depth = depth;
